        "role": "user",
        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - Math problem with drawings (start)] The whiteboard shows drawings and a math problem written in light blue. On the left are drawings resembling base-ten blocks: a rectangle with \"60\" inside it, and two vertical lines next to it. Above this, \"100\" is written with a \"1\" above it. In the center of the board, the following is written: \"+(15x2)\", \"+ 30\", and \"= 90\". There is a small, incomplete black drawing of a rectangle below \"(15x2)\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification appears at the top of the screen: \"Miguel has joined\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 303 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 57 seconds] The whiteboard shows some math work in light blue and black marker. On the left, there are drawings of tens and ones blocks. On the right, it says \"+(15x2)\", \"+ 30\", and \"= 90\". The student's video feed is a red circle with the letter 'M'.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification \"Diana has joined\" appears. The screen changes to a dark background with the student's initial 'M' in a red circle.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The screen layout changes to show two video feeds. The student's feed is on the left, showing a red circle with the letter 'M'. The tutor's feed is on the right, showing a green circle with the letter 'V'.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 1 second]\nTurn 1. STUDENT: Hi.\nTurn 2. TUTOR: Hi. How are you?\nTurn 3. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 3 seconds]\nTurn 3. TUTOR: Did anything exciting happen today?\nTurn 4. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 4 seconds]\nTurn 4. TUTOR: Anything you're looking forward to next week?\nTurn 5. STUDENT: Next week I have school.\nTurn 6. TUTOR: Really?\nTurn 7. STUDENT: Yes.\nTurn 8. TUTOR: Your school starts really early.\nTurn 9. STUDENT: Are you already past like two weeks?\nTurn 10. TUTOR: My school starts, um, in August, on August eighteenth.\nTurn 11. STUDENT: The mine August, August first.\nTurn 12. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 6 seconds]\nTurn 12. TUTOR: Oh, okay.\nTurn 13. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 5 seconds]\nTurn 13. TUTOR: Give me one second.\nTurn 14. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 14 seconds]\nTurn 14. TUTOR: All right. Um, I'm going to share my screen and then we can start on problems for today.\nTurn 15. TUTOR: Um, okay.\nTurn 16. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds]\nTurn 16. TUTOR: Can you see my screen?\nTurn 17. STUDENT: Yes.\nTurn 18. TUTOR: Okay, why don't you read, um, the problem out loud.\nTurn 19. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 9 seconds]\nTurn 19. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'A grasshopper starts at 0cm...' (start)] The tutor shares her screen, showing