9. STUDENT: Thirty cm.\nTurn 40. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 4 seconds]\nTurn 40. TUTOR: What do you mean by that?\nTurn 41. STUDENT: D.\nTurn 42. TUTOR: I think your answer of A was correct. I just want to know how you got that answer because it's the right one, but I want to double-check your thinking process.\nTurn 43. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 3 seconds]\nTurn 43. STUDENT: Because\nTurn 44. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds]\nTurn 44. STUDENT: like\nTurn 45. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 12 seconds]\nTurn 45. STUDENT: Because I count one, two, three, four.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nPush for appropriate rigor now. Build on what the learner has actually demonstrated, not an answer the tutor supplied. Invite the student to justify a step, connect representations, check a claim, generalize, or take the next step independently. Choose one opportunity that fits the current problem. Do not give the reasoning before asking for it, turn the challenge into a sequence of tiny leading hints, or introduce unrelated difficulty. Keep the mathematical work with the learner; rigor is not simply saying why after every answer.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
